PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I'm still leaning toward N after the round-1 exchange. Statement I remains the clearest positive: every standard twitching protocol uses stab inoculation through 1 % agar to place cells at the agar–plastic interface, and the subsurface halo is the accepted readout. On III, multiple motility papers explicitly describe P. aeruginosa swarming on 0.5–0.7 % agar with glycerol plus ammonium salts, so the carbon-source requirement is met without needing casamino acids or glutamate.  

The volume figure in II looks overstated—most published swarm and twitch plates use closer to 10 ml per 10 cm dish to keep the layer thin enough for interface migration, which undercuts the 25 ml claim. IV is assay-dependent rather than absolute; iron chelators can suppress or enhance swarming depending on the precise medium and iron background, so the blanket “inhibit” wording does not hold as a general rule. V is ruled out because two washes strip the diffusible pigments and leave a white pellet.  

Thus only I and III survive across the common published conditions, which still points to N rather than the broader consensus around C.}

