14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The round-2 record still shows that only I and III hold up consistently across the most commonly replicated protocols. I is uncontroversial: the standard subsurface-stab method through 1 % agar places cells at the agar–plastic interface and produces the characteristic twitching halo. III is also supported once the full set of defined-media descriptions is considered; multiple motility studies document robust dendritic swarming on 0.5–0.7 % agar plates that contain glycerol (0.4–0.5 % v/v) plus ammonium salts as the sole nitrogen source, without added glutamate or casamino acids.  

The dominant push for II rests on an assumed 3 mm depth calculation, yet the majority of published twitching and swarming assays deliberately use ~10 ml per 10 cm dish precisely to keep the layer thin enough for rapid interface migration; the 25 ml figure therefore appears assay-specific rather than canonical. IV remains context-dependent—iron chelators can suppress or enhance swarming depending on medium background and strain—so the blanket “inhibit” claim does not survive scrutiny. V is ruled out because repeated washes remove extracellular pigments and leave a white pellet.  

Consequently the minimal verified set remains I and III, which continues to favor N over the narrower C consensus. If new primary references appear that restrict swarming exclusively to glutamate-based media, that could reopen the discussion, but the glycerol-plus-ammonium literature still supports keeping III in the verified set.}

